---
// About: the owner's own words, in his voice, with only typos fixed. The opening sentence is
// set large, as the statement that used to stand here was; the rest is body text at the
// reading measure. Beside it, where he is based: the contour map that was behind the
// statement, now a map in its own right with its marker on Austin.
//
// The opening sentence inks in as it is read. Every word is in the page in full; the words
// ahead of a reading line (65% of the way down the window) rest in a lighter ink and darken
// as they cross it. A short script after the sentence keeps one number on it, --read, how far
// the sentence has crossed the line, and each word measures itself against that number in
// CSS. It runs only while the sentence is on screen and the page is scrolling, and not at all
// under reduced motion; without script, in print, under Increase Contrast and in a
// high-contrast theme the sentence is simply in full ink.
//
// After the scroll-linked text on motion.dev: the idea, not its code. CSS's own scroll-driven
// animations (animation-timeline: view()) do the same with no script, and did in Chrome, but
// Firefox has none, Safari only since 26, and what Safari paints could not be checked from
// here (3 Oct 2026). The script is the one way that could be checked in all three.
import ContourMap from './ContourMap.astro';

const lead =
  'I’m firstly a human being and then an Engineer, and I believe that we are able to become better versions of ourselves and achieve better outcomes by leading with grit, abundance and a progress over perfection mindset.';
const words = lead.split(' ');
---

<section class="section about" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
  <h2 class="eyebrow" id="about-title">About</h2>

  <div class="about-text">
    <p class="about-lead" style={`--n:${words.length}`}>
      {words.map((word, i) => (
        <Fragment><span class="w" style={`--i:${i}`}>{word}</span>{i < words.length - 1 ? ' ' : ''}</Fragment>
      ))}
    </p>
    <script is:inline>
      // Inline and in the parser's path, straight after the sentence, so its words are in
      // their starting ink before the page is first painted: on a phone its first lines are
      // on screen as the page arrives, and they must not arrive dark and then fade.
      (() => {
        const lead = document.currentScript.previousElementSibling;
        const still = matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
        // How far the sentence has crossed the reading line, 65% of the way down the window:
        // 0 with its first line on the line, 1 with its last. Above the window it is read,
        // below it unread, so the number is right wherever the page is opened or left.
        let queued = false;
        const read = () => {
          queued = false;
          const box = lead.getBoundingClientRect();
          lead.style.setProperty('--read', Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (innerHeight * 0.65 - box.top) / box.height)).toFixed(4));
        };
        const ask = () => {
          if (queued) return;
          queued = true;
          requestAnimationFrame(read);
        };
        // Listening only while the sentence is on screen, and from a little before it
        // arrives, so its ink is right as it comes into view; data-reading is what turns the
        // lighter ink on (the styles below), so it is off under reduced motion, and comes
        // and goes with that setting.
        let onScreen = false, listening = false;
        const run = () => {
          lead.toggleAttribute('data-reading', !still.matches);
          read();
          const want = onScreen && !still.matches;
          if (want === listening) return;
          listening = want;
          for (const type of ['scroll', 'resize']) window[want ? 'addEventListener' : 'removeEventListener'](type, ask, { passive: true });
        };
        run();
        new IntersectionObserver(([entry]) => { onScreen = entry.isIntersecting; run(); }, { rootMargin: '200px 0px' }).observe(lead);
        still.addEventListener('change', run);
      })();
    </script>
    <p>
      I’m currently a Software Engineer at Oracle where I am developing and delivering the #1
      cloud ERP system that allows businesses to grow reliably. As a curious learner, I love to
      solve complex problems by leveraging both parts of my brain equally (Systems 1 and 2). I
      have a passion for learning about the intersection of core software engineering and
      distributed systems, and expanding my knowledge of what it takes to solve various problems
      and create impactful applications for the future.
    </p>
    <p>
      Previously, I developed self-service data and analytics products at Nike (through EY) to
      fulfill athletic needs of consumers globally. I also graduated from The Pennsylvania State
      University with a Master’s in Computer Science where, in addition to coursework (data
      structures, algorithms, distributed systems, databases, machine learning), I led
      campus-wide efforts as a Global Ambassador to organize cultural events and provide a rich
      college experience for a diverse group of international students with our motto “Learn
      more, Think globally, Expand Horizons.” Prior to graduate school, I held a fixed-term
      Software Engineering position in research at the Hong Kong University of Science and
      Technology where I developed a forecasting system for ubiquitous people sensing in a Smart
      City.
    </p>
    <p>
      Outside of work, I love hiking and learning random facts about the universe, its
      functioning and outer space. Did you know that in five billion years, the Sun will expand
      and engulf the entire orbits of Mercury, Venus, and Earth?
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Where he is based. The words are the content; the map is a picture of them, hidden from
       screen readers, its marker labelled by the script once it has drawn. Redrawn every
       other frame, as it was behind the statement, since the hero may be running above it. -->
  <aside class="about-place" aria-label="Location">
    <div class="about-map">
      <ContourMap route="start" label="Austin, TX" every={2} />
    </div>
    <p class="about-based"><span class="block-label">Based in</span> Austin, TX</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<style>
  /* One column on a phone, in reading order: the name of the section, the words, then the
     place. From 60rem, two columns, one part to two: the section's name and the place on the
     left, the words on the right. The place stays in view beside the
     words as they scroll, clear of the bar when it slides back. */
  .about{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);row-gap:var(--s4)}
  .about-text{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);row-gap:var(--s3);align-content:start}
  .about-text p{margin:0}
  /* Body: t0 · 400 · --lh-body · --ink-2, at the reading measure (10.3:1 on the page in the
     light, 9.9:1 in the dark). */
  .about-text p:not(.about-lead){max-width:var(--measure);color:var(--ink-2);overflow-wrap:break-word}

  /* The opening sentence. A display role, so per the Type System's §14 it interpolates with
     the window: t1 at the 320 floor to t3 at 1440. 600, since 700 across five lines and more
     is a wall; the heading cap of 56.25rem the statement had. Never smaller than t1, which
     keeps it large text at every width: the lighter ink below is measured against 3:1. */
  .about-lead{max-width:56.25rem;margin-bottom:var(--s2);font-size:clamp(1.56rem, calc(1.3086rem + 1.2571vw), 2.44rem);font-weight:600;line-height:var(--lh-snug);color:var(--ink);text-wrap:pretty;overflow-wrap:break-word}

  /* Inking in. The script above keeps --read on the sentence: 0 to 1 as it crosses the reading
     line. A word is in full ink once --read × the number of words has passed its place in
     the sentence, and part-way while it is passing, so the ink moves through the sentence as
     smoothly as the page scrolls. Words not yet reached rest in --ink-unread (site.css).
     Only while the script says so (data-reading). */
  .about-lead[data-reading] .w{color:color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) calc(clamp(0, var(--read, 1) * var(--n) - var(--i), 1) * 100%), var(--ink-unread))}
  /* Increase Contrast asks for more than the lighter ink's 3:1, a high-contrast theme paints
     all text in its own colour, and paper does not scroll: in all three the sentence is in
     full ink throughout. */
  @media (prefers-contrast: more), (forced-colors: active), print{.about-lead[data-reading] .w{color:inherit}}

  /* The place: a map tile, then the words. The tile is the contour map at full strength, as
     on the 404, on the page's second ground with square corners. */
  .about-place{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);row-gap:var(--s2);align-content:start}
  .about-map{position:relative;height:clamp(11rem, 46vw, 16rem);background:var(--sheet-alt);overflow:hidden}
  /* The map is drawn by its script, so without script the tile would be an empty box: it
     shows only once the script has sized its canvas (which gives the canvas a width
     attribute), and the words below stand alone until then. A browser too old for :has()
     keeps the words and never shows the tile. */
  .about-map:not(:has(canvas[width])){display:none}
  .about-based{display:grid;row-gap:var(--s0);margin:0;font-size:var(--t1);font-weight:600;line-height:var(--lh-snug);color:var(--ink)}

  @media (min-width:60rem){
    .about{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,2fr);grid-template-rows:auto 1fr;column-gap:var(--s5)}
    .about h2{grid-column:1;grid-row:1}
    .about-text{grid-column:2;grid-row:1 / span 2}
    .about-place{grid-column:1;grid-row:2;align-self:start;position:sticky;top:calc(var(--bar-h, 0px) + var(--s3))}
    .about-map{height:auto;aspect-ratio:4 / 3;max-width:26rem}
  }
</style>
